\pard\tx360\tx720\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\fi360\sl264\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs26 Over the next few days, Sarah devoted her spare time and spare thoughts spaces to Cracker. She risked the trip to his apartment twice a day, so she could feed, clothe, and bathe him (boxer shorts resolutely stayed on), and tend to his wounds with Airborne bandages, creams, unguents, and toenail clippers. She filled his freezer with microwave meals. She bought him new socks, cologne and vodka he could actually drink. She subscribed him to the best on-demand Xtreme Fighting Porn, and he marvelled at the tag-team doggy hammer throws. Although scars remained encamped on his face, hands and chest, his health and demeanour improved. \
When Sarah felt his wounds were settled enough, she thought it would improve his appearance if she could exfoliate all the flaking dead skin away. One night she sat him on bath\'92s edge, naked but for his boxer shorts, and produced a purple-black cloth which she used to buff his head.\
\'93Ow!\'94 Cracker complained.\
\'93Men are such babies,\'94 Sarah scolded. \'93Look, it\'92s working.\'94\
Cracker checked out his noggin in the mirror, and was pleased with the result. He rubbed his hand over his scalp and enjoyed the smoother feel where Sarah had worked. He submitted to her resumption. \'93What is that cloth anyway?\'94\
\'93Giraffe tongue.\'94\
Cracker pushed her away. \'93What the AF? Aren\'92t they extinct?\'94\
\'93Well they are now,\'94 Sarah said as she rubbed behind his ears.\
\'93Great. You\'92re a giraffe exterminator. Is there anything you Airborne won\'92t kill?\'94\
\'93Well, I didn\'92t kill it. My grandmother left me this when I was a child. It has great sentimental value.\'94\
\'93Really?\'94\
\'93Yeah. She used to polish her bowling balls with it.\'94\
When Sarah finished, Cracker proudly modelled for her. She applauded. He felt reinvigorated.\
\'93Hey, I\'92m gonna take a shower. What\'92s say we go out for a couple of drinks? I\'92m dying to splash on some of that new vodka you got me.\'94\
Sarah looked at him askance. \'93Are you asking me on a date?\'94\
Cracker blushed and shrugged. \'93Nah, just some karaoke, you know \'85\'94\
\'93At an ELVIS bar?\'94\
\'93Of course. What other kind is there?\'94\
Sarah beamed as fully as if he asked her on a weekend riverboat cruise.\

